{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims the preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible via 'verisimilitude' without relying on probability or induction. However, this defense collapses because 'verisimilitude' itself cannot be operationalized as a guide for future action without an inductive assumption. To prefer Theory A over Theory B because A has higher verisimilitude based on past tests implies the belief that the structural features which allowed A to survive past tests will continue to make it closer to the truth in future, untested applications. This is precisely the 'uniformity of nature' assumption that Popper rejected. If one does not assume the future resembles the past, the fact that a theory survived severe tests yesterday provides no logical leverage for expecting it to be a better guide tomorrow than a falsified theory or an untested one. Thus, invoking verisimilitude as a pragmatic decision rule merely disguises the inductive inference it purports to avoid; it asserts that past success tracks truth-likeness, which is an inductive claim about the relationship between testing history and ontological status.","counterexample":["Consider two theories: T1 (survived 100 severe tests) and T2 (falsified once). On Popper's terms, T1 has high corroboration, T2 has none. The target says we prefer T1 due to higher verisimilitude. But if the universe changes its laws tomorrow (no uniformity), T1's past survival is irrelevant to its future truth-likeness. Without assuming the future resembles the past, preferring T1 is irrational, proving the preference relies on the very induction Popper banned."],"premise":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a non-inductive guide for action, but this concept fails to provide a rational basis for preference without smuggling in induction.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be measured independently of past performance, how can it serve as a non-inductive bridge to future reliability?","target":"e2f347c0859b96e1f05e5bfe2c293ebbc9f7ec206a954425931fd983a41fc3ee"}]}